  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "In the quadrilateral $ABCD$, the points $A$, $B$ and $D$ are at $(3,3)$, $(0,1)$ and $(6,2)$, respectively. The line $BD$ bisects the line $AC$ at right-angles at the point $M$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the equation of $BD$ and of $AC$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$BD: 6y-x=6$; $AC: y+6x=21$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{BD}&=\\frac{2-1}{6-0}\\\\m_{BD}&=\\frac16\\end{align*}$$\nThrough $B(0,1)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y-1&=\\frac16(x-0)\\\\6y-x&=6\\end{align*}$$\nSince $AC\\perp BD$, $m_{AC}=-6$. Through $A(3,3)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y-3&=-6(x-3)\\\\y-3&=-6x+18\\\\y+6x&=21\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Calculate the coordinates of $M$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$M\\left(\\frac{120}{37},\\frac{57}{37}\\right)$.",
          "worked": "$M$ lies on both $BD$ and $AC$. Substitute $y=21-6x$ into $6y-x=6$:\n$$\\begin{align*}6(21-6x)-x&=6\\\\126-37x&=6\\\\37x&=120\\\\x&=\\frac{120}{37}\\\\y&=21-6\\left(\\frac{120}{37}\\right)\\\\y&=\\frac{57}{37}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $M=\\left(\\frac{120}{37},\\frac{57}{37}\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Calculate the length $AM$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$AM=\\frac{9\\sqrt{37}}{37}$ units.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}AM^2&=\\left(\\frac{120}{37}-3\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{57}{37}-3\\right)^2\\\\AM^2&=\\left(\\frac9{37}\\right)^2+\\left(-\\frac{54}{37}\\right)^2\\\\AM^2&=\\frac{2997}{1369}\\\\AM&=\\frac{9\\sqrt{37}}{37}\\text{ units}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find the area of quadrilateral $ABCD$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$9$ square units.\n\nUse $BD$ as the common base of triangles $ABD$ and $CBD$; their perpendicular heights are equal because $AM=CM$.",
          "worked": "Since $BD$ bisects $AC$ at right-angles, $AM=CM$ and both are perpendicular heights to base $BD$.\n$$\\begin{align*}BD^2&=(6-0)^2+(2-1)^2\\\\BD^2&=37\\\\BD&=\\sqrt{37}\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Area of }ABCD&=\\frac12 BD\\times AM+\\frac12 BD\\times CM\\\\\\text{Area of }ABCD&=BD\\times AM\\\\\\text{Area of }ABCD&=\\sqrt{37}\\times\\frac{9\\sqrt{37}}{37}\\\\\\text{Area of }ABCD&=9\\text{ square units}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
